# D-269, and the first thing the generator found on a clean tree.
#
# Written by `lineage_property_tests.rs` and promoted by hand — the history
# below is verbatim what the shrinker produced, the comment is not.
#
# `x` forks. The trunk then restates edges it already holds, at the same keys
# and the same intervals, which is what an idempotent importer does on every
# run. One ordinary `archive` follows. Every restated log entry is superseded on
# its own lineage, so `LOG_ARCHIVABLE` took it — and `x` reads its ancestors
# through `recorded_at <= forked_at`, so the entry it was pinned to is the one
# that went cold. The branch loses `a → b` while the trunk keeps it.
#
# **No branch writes anything anywhere in this history.** That is what makes it
# a different finding from D-229 rather than another spelling of it: D-229
# needed a lineage disagreeing at a shared key, and this needs only a lineage
# that existed while somebody wrote twice.
#
# The clause that fixes it is the `NOT EXISTS (… branches.forked_at …)` guard on
# `LOG_ARCHIVABLE` in `src/temporal/archive.rs`. Remove it and this case fails.
fork parent=main child=x
assert key=1 branch=main closed=0
assert key=0 branch=main closed=0
assert key=0 branch=main closed=0
assert key=0 branch=main closed=0
archive
